    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d710">

                <pb facs="1002" n="992" sameAs="frus:frus1945v07/pg992"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 P.W./1–1745: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Hurley</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-01-17T18:00:00+07:00">January 17, 1945—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received January 17—12:35 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>80. Consul [at] Tihwa reports following based on interviews January 12 with
                    General Chu: Insurgents now number about 15,000; they show evidence of military
                    training and are generously supplied with mortars, machine guns and ammunition.
                    Situation of Chinese garrisoning in airfield desperate. Huiyuan fell to
                    insurgents on December 25 and Suiting December 30. Chinese troops retiring to
                    point on Horgos–Tihwa highway between Chingho and Hsin Erhtai.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hurley</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
